\documentclass{article}
\title{Rebalancing Bike-Share Networks Under Commuter Tidal Flows}
\author{M. Ferreira and D. Osei}
\date{June 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Bike-share systems in dense cities empty and refill in tidal waves:
docks near offices saturate by nine in the morning,
while residential docks empty over the same window.
We study how much rebalancing effort is needed to keep both ends usable
across a full working week.

\section{Demand Model}
Trips are modelled as a doubly stochastic process
with origin demand tied to residential density
and destination demand tied to office floor space.
Calibration on one spring month shows that ridership grows like \sqrt{1.4}$
whenever dock density near a workplace doubles,
which makes dense office clusters disproportionately expensive to serve.
The model is refitted weekly so that seasonal drift does not accumulate.

\section{Dock Survey}
We surveyed every dock in the three pilot districts.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
District & Docks surveyed \\
\hline
Northbank & 34 \\
Old Market & 27 & 41 \\
South Quay & 52 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Old Market has the fewest docks but the highest turnover at midday.

\section{Rebalancing}
The operator dispatches two vans each morning
to move bikes from saturated office docks back to residential areas.
Dispatch follows a simple threshold rule:
any dock above ninety percent full is queued for pickup.
We show the rule removes most morning outages
at roughly half the cost of a full optimisation pass.

\section{Conclusion}
Tidal flows dominate the failure modes of the network,
and a threshold rule captures most of the benefit
that a complex dispatch optimiser would deliver.
\end{document}

