while residential docks empty over the same window.
We study how much rebalancing effort is needed to keep both ends usable
across a full working week.

\section{Demand Model}
Trips are modelled as a doubly stochastic process
with origin demand tied to residential density
and destination demand tied to office floor space.
Calibration on one spring month shows that ridership grows like $\sqrt{1.4}$
whenever dock density near a workplace doubles,
which makes dense office clusters disproportionately expensive to serve.
The model is refitted weekly so that seasonal drift does not accumulate.

\section{Dock Survey}
We surveyed every dock in the three pilot districts.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
District & Docks surveyed \\
\hline
Northbank & 34 \\
Old Market & 27 \\
South Quay & 52 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
